1
00:00:00,680 --> 00:00:03,877
At five in the morning on December eighteenth,

2
00:00:03,897 --> 00:00:07,497
twenty twenty three, more than two thousand police officers

3
00:00:07,517 --> 00:00:10,714
and soldiers surrounded a prison in Paraguay's capital.

4
00:00:12,034 --> 00:00:14,828
They were not there to stop an escape.

5
00:00:14,848 --> 00:00:17,643
They were there to take the prison back.

6
00:00:17,663 --> 00:00:20,457
Because for years, according to the government itself,

7
00:00:20,477 --> 00:00:23,623
the people really in charge inside were not the

8
00:00:23,643 --> 00:00:23,975
guards.

9
00:00:25,295 --> 00:00:26,930
They were a gang.

10
00:00:26,950 --> 00:00:30,655
The Rotela Clan. And by the time the operation

11
00:00:30,675 --> 00:00:32,724
was over, people were dead,

12
00:00:32,744 --> 00:00:35,207
hundreds of prisoners were on buses,

13
00:00:35,227 --> 00:00:37,690
and the state had found weapons,

14
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:40,173
explosives, and, in an earlier search,

15
00:00:40,193 --> 00:00:41,415
a fighting rooster.

16
00:00:43,335 --> 00:00:45,992
How does a country lose control of its biggest

17
00:00:46,012 --> 00:00:48,372
prison, in the middle of its own capital?

18
00:00:48,392 --> 00:00:50,752
And what happened after it took it back?

19
00:00:53,292 --> 00:00:56,100
Chapter 1. A quiet country.

20
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:03,142
Paraguay rarely makes international headlines.

21
00:01:03,162 --> 00:01:07,573
It is a landlocked country of around seven million

22
00:01:07,593 --> 00:01:11,020
people, in the heart of South America,

23
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:13,482
between Argentina, Brazil and Bolivia.

24
00:01:14,802 --> 00:01:16,494
But its location matters.

25
00:01:16,514 --> 00:01:20,348
Paraguay sits on routes used to move cocaine from

26
00:01:20,368 --> 00:01:22,061
the Andes toward Brazil,

27
00:01:22,081 --> 00:01:25,059
Argentina, and the ports of the Atlantic.

28
00:01:25,079 --> 00:01:28,056
It is also, according to many estimates,

29
00:01:28,076 --> 00:01:31,910
one of the largest producers of marijuana in South

30
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:32,338
America.

31
00:01:33,658 --> 00:01:35,758
And where drugs move,

32
00:01:35,778 --> 00:01:40,528
gangs follow. The most powerful is not even Paraguayan.

33
00:01:40,548 --> 00:01:43,178
The Primeiro Comando da Capital,

34
00:01:43,198 --> 00:01:46,888
or PCC, is Brazil's largest criminal organization.

35
00:01:46,908 --> 00:01:49,008
Over the past decade,

36
00:01:49,028 --> 00:01:52,188
it has pushed deep into Paraguay.

37
00:01:53,508 --> 00:01:57,005
The violence reached even the people investigating it.

38
00:01:57,025 --> 00:01:59,202
In May twenty twenty two,

39
00:01:59,222 --> 00:02:01,840
Paraguay's top anti organized crime prosecutor,

40
00:02:01,860 --> 00:02:05,795
Marcelo Pecci, was shot dead on a beach in

41
00:02:05,815 --> 00:02:07,993
Colombia, while on his honeymoon.

42
00:02:08,013 --> 00:02:11,949
For many Paraguayans, it was the moment they realized

43
00:02:11,969 --> 00:02:14,586
how far organized crime had come.

44
00:02:15,906 --> 00:02:19,216
Much of that expansion happened in prisons.

45
00:02:19,236 --> 00:02:21,119
In January twenty twenty,

46
00:02:21,139 --> 00:02:25,401
dozens of PCC members escaped from a prison in

47
00:02:25,421 --> 00:02:28,731
Pedro Juan Caballero, on the Brazilian border,

48
00:02:28,751 --> 00:02:32,537
through a tunnel. Officials suspected help from inside.

49
00:02:33,857 --> 00:02:37,742
Paraguay's prisons were an easy place for gangs to

50
00:02:37,762 --> 00:02:41,647
grow. According to official data cited by InSight Crime,

51
00:02:41,667 --> 00:02:45,118
the system held more than eighteen thousand people,

52
00:02:45,138 --> 00:02:48,156
in space built for around ten thousand.

53
00:02:48,176 --> 00:02:51,193
About two thirds had never been convicted.

54
00:02:51,213 --> 00:02:53,363
They were waiting for trial.

55
00:02:54,683 --> 00:02:58,582
And the biggest, most crowded prison of all was

56
00:02:58,602 --> 00:03:00,323
in the capital, Asunción.

57
00:03:00,343 --> 00:03:02,065
Its name was Tacumbú.

58
00:03:04,405 --> 00:03:07,132
Chapter 2. Tacumbú.

59
00:03:11,732 --> 00:03:15,881
The National Penitentiary of Tacumbú sits in the Tacumbú

60
00:03:15,901 --> 00:03:19,586
neighborhood of Asunción. For more than sixty years,

61
00:03:19,606 --> 00:03:22,365
it was the country's main prison.

62
00:03:23,685 --> 00:03:25,579
By twenty twenty three,

63
00:03:25,599 --> 00:03:27,492
it was badly overcrowded.

64
00:03:27,512 --> 00:03:31,798
Paraguay's president put its population at around two thousand

65
00:03:31,818 --> 00:03:36,104
seven hundred. Other reports said more than three thousand.

66
00:03:36,124 --> 00:03:39,452
Nobody seemed to know the exact number.

67
00:03:40,772 --> 00:03:43,580
Inside, according to researchers and local media,

68
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:46,004
the prison was divided into pavilions.

69
00:03:46,024 --> 00:03:48,023
And in many of them,

70
00:03:48,043 --> 00:03:51,255
the authority that mattered was not the warden.

71
00:03:52,575 --> 00:03:55,813
It was a gang that had started out on

72
00:03:55,833 --> 00:03:57,623
the streets, selling crack cocaine.

73
00:03:57,643 --> 00:03:59,072
And it had found,

74
00:03:59,092 --> 00:04:01,606
in Tacumbú, the perfect place to grow.

75
00:04:03,946 --> 00:04:06,290
Chapter 3. The clan.

76
00:04:10,890 --> 00:04:14,641
The Rotela Clan is named after its leader,

77
00:04:14,661 --> 00:04:17,941
Armando Javier Rotela. According to Paraguayan media,

78
00:04:17,961 --> 00:04:21,241
the group formed around two thousand seven,

79
00:04:21,261 --> 00:04:25,484
and built its business on selling crack in Asunción

80
00:04:25,504 --> 00:04:26,898
and other cities.

81
00:04:28,218 --> 00:04:31,207
Rotela was first arrested in twenty eleven.

82
00:04:31,227 --> 00:04:33,356
He escaped in twenty twelve.

83
00:04:33,376 --> 00:04:36,365
He was caught again in twenty sixteen,

84
00:04:36,385 --> 00:04:38,944
in the south of the country.

85
00:04:38,964 --> 00:04:42,812
In twenty twenty, he was sentenced to nineteen years

86
00:04:42,832 --> 00:04:43,672
in prison.

87
00:04:44,992 --> 00:04:47,714
Prison did not stop the clan.

88
00:04:47,734 --> 00:04:50,913
It changed it. According to InSight Crime,

89
00:04:50,933 --> 00:04:55,027
the Rotela Clan was not originally a prison gang.

90
00:04:55,047 --> 00:04:57,312
But inside an overcrowded system,

91
00:04:57,332 --> 00:05:00,054
it found a new business model.

92
00:05:00,074 --> 00:05:00,511
Protection.

93
00:05:01,831 --> 00:05:05,038
New inmates arrived in a place where the state

94
00:05:05,058 --> 00:05:06,831
could not keep them safe.

95
00:05:06,851 --> 00:05:09,341
The clan could, in exchange for loyalty.

96
00:05:10,661 --> 00:05:14,176
By twenty twenty, according to Paraguayan security officials,

97
00:05:14,196 --> 00:05:18,154
the clan had somewhere between one thousand and four

98
00:05:18,174 --> 00:05:21,689
thousand members. Many of them inside the walls.

99
00:05:23,009 --> 00:05:26,068
According to prosecutors cited by La Nación,

100
00:05:26,088 --> 00:05:27,828
the clan supplied drugs,

101
00:05:27,848 --> 00:05:30,467
and weapons for hitmen and extortion.

102
00:05:30,487 --> 00:05:34,427
Many of its members were young men held without

103
00:05:34,447 --> 00:05:37,946
trial, repeat offenders, or people struggling with addiction.

104
00:05:37,966 --> 00:05:41,905
Exactly the kind of people Paraguay's prisons were full

105
00:05:41,925 --> 00:05:42,345
of.

106
00:05:43,665 --> 00:05:45,400
A Paraguayan security expert,

107
00:05:45,420 --> 00:05:49,349
Jorge Rolón Luna, described their position in Tacumbú plainly.

108
00:05:49,369 --> 00:05:51,104
They are the majority,

109
00:05:51,124 --> 00:05:55,053
he said, and they have agreements and pacts with

110
00:05:55,073 --> 00:05:57,247
the people who run Tacumbú.

111
00:05:58,567 --> 00:06:01,560
But the clan had a dangerous enemy.

112
00:06:01,580 --> 00:06:02,420
The PCC.

113
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:07,978
Chapter 4. The war inside.

114
00:06:12,578 --> 00:06:15,087
The two gangs wanted the same thing.

115
00:06:15,107 --> 00:06:16,533
Control of Paraguay's prisons,

116
00:06:16,553 --> 00:06:19,786
and of the drug trade that ran through them.

117
00:06:21,106 --> 00:06:22,814
In June twenty nineteen,

118
00:06:22,834 --> 00:06:26,702
the war exploded in a prison in San Pedro,

119
00:06:26,722 --> 00:06:29,294
in the north. During a riot,

120
00:06:29,314 --> 00:06:33,183
PCC prisoners killed ten members of the Rotela Clan.

121
00:06:33,203 --> 00:06:35,775
Some of the victims were beheaded.

122
00:06:37,095 --> 00:06:39,350
In the capital, at Tacumbú,

123
00:06:39,370 --> 00:06:41,626
the Rotela Clan remained dominant.

124
00:06:42,946 --> 00:06:46,301
According to critics, an arrangement held for years.

125
00:06:46,321 --> 00:06:48,411
The government had a prison.

126
00:06:48,431 --> 00:06:50,521
The clan had a base.

127
00:06:51,841 --> 00:06:53,763
Then, in twenty twenty three,

128
00:06:53,783 --> 00:06:56,093
a new government came to power.

129
00:06:56,113 --> 00:06:58,422
And the arrangement started to break.

130
00:07:00,762 --> 00:07:03,106
Chapter 5. The siege.

131
00:07:07,706 --> 00:07:10,526
In August twenty twenty three,

132
00:07:10,546 --> 00:07:13,934
Santiago Peña became president of Paraguay.

133
00:07:13,954 --> 00:07:17,910
Weeks later, an inmate disappeared from Tacumbú.

134
00:07:19,230 --> 00:07:21,475
His name was Óliver Lezcano,

135
00:07:21,495 --> 00:07:23,287
a former police officer.

136
00:07:23,307 --> 00:07:27,364
He went missing inside the prison at the end

137
00:07:27,384 --> 00:07:29,629
of September. On October second,

138
00:07:29,649 --> 00:07:33,706
the justice minister publicly blamed the Rotela Clan for

139
00:07:33,726 --> 00:07:35,518
kidnapping and killing him.

140
00:07:35,538 --> 00:07:38,689
The clan claimed he had simply escaped.

141
00:07:40,009 --> 00:07:42,756
The government began moving against the clan.

142
00:07:42,776 --> 00:07:44,337
And on October tenth,

143
00:07:44,357 --> 00:07:45,522
the clan answered.

144
00:07:46,842 --> 00:07:48,619
Prisoners took over Tacumbú.

145
00:07:48,639 --> 00:07:50,865
According to the justice ministry,

146
00:07:50,885 --> 00:07:54,909
they held at least ten guards and around thirty

147
00:07:54,929 --> 00:07:58,952
female visitors hostage. Local media reported that the prison's

148
00:07:58,972 --> 00:08:01,198
director was among those held.

149
00:08:02,518 --> 00:08:04,426
In less than a day,

150
00:08:04,446 --> 00:08:07,126
it was over. The hostages were released.

151
00:08:07,146 --> 00:08:09,055
But the message was clear.

152
00:08:09,075 --> 00:08:12,526
The clan could take the prison whenever it wanted.

153
00:08:14,646 --> 00:08:18,053
President Peña refused to call it a crisis.

154
00:08:18,073 --> 00:08:19,766
It was, he said,

155
00:08:19,786 --> 00:08:22,336
part of a confrontation with crime.

156
00:08:22,356 --> 00:08:25,763
A confrontation the government now had to win.

157
00:08:27,303 --> 00:08:29,948
Chapter 6. The rooster.

158
00:08:34,548 --> 00:08:36,652
Over the following weeks,

159
00:08:36,672 --> 00:08:40,368
the government prepared. Searches were carried out.

160
00:08:40,388 --> 00:08:41,961
Intelligence was gathered.

161
00:08:43,281 --> 00:08:46,353
On December thirteenth, during one search,

162
00:08:46,373 --> 00:08:49,446
officials seized drugs and homemade weapons.

163
00:08:49,466 --> 00:08:51,507
And, according to Infobae,

164
00:08:51,527 --> 00:08:56,146
a fighting rooster. The kind of bird raised for

165
00:08:56,166 --> 00:08:56,661
cockfights.

166
00:08:57,981 --> 00:08:59,882
It was a small detail.

167
00:08:59,902 --> 00:09:03,339
But it said a lot about who had really

168
00:09:03,359 --> 00:09:05,259
been living how, inside Tacumbú.

169
00:09:07,179 --> 00:09:10,073
Five days later, the government made its move.

170
00:09:12,613 --> 00:09:16,280
Chapter 7. Operation Veneratio.

171
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:24,679
At five in the morning on December eighteenth,

172
00:09:24,699 --> 00:09:27,543
twenty twenty three, the operation began.

173
00:09:27,563 --> 00:09:31,838
According to Infobae, one thousand one hundred police officers

174
00:09:31,858 --> 00:09:36,134
and one thousand two hundred and eighteen soldiers took

175
00:09:36,154 --> 00:09:38,998
part. It was called Operation Veneratio.

176
00:09:40,318 --> 00:09:43,367
The forces moved pavilion by pavilion.

177
00:09:43,387 --> 00:09:46,436
The hardest target was Pavilion D.

178
00:09:46,456 --> 00:09:48,993
Inmates called it La Jungla.

179
00:09:49,013 --> 00:09:52,574
The jungle. That was where Rotela lived.

180
00:09:53,894 --> 00:09:58,242
There was resistance. A police officer was killed with

181
00:09:58,262 --> 00:10:01,154
a machete. Several inmates were killed.

182
00:10:01,174 --> 00:10:05,522
By midday, officials put the toll at six dead.

183
00:10:05,542 --> 00:10:08,435
Some later reports gave higher numbers.

184
00:10:08,455 --> 00:10:11,347
At least twelve people were injured.

185
00:10:12,667 --> 00:10:14,268
According to the government,

186
00:10:14,288 --> 00:10:16,295
Rotela did not go quietly.

187
00:10:16,315 --> 00:10:19,943
A vice minister said he tried to use a

188
00:10:19,963 --> 00:10:21,970
pregnant woman as a shield.

189
00:10:21,990 --> 00:10:23,592
She was not hurt.

190
00:10:23,612 --> 00:10:25,619
That account comes from officials,

191
00:10:25,639 --> 00:10:28,051
and could not be independently verified.

192
00:10:29,371 --> 00:10:31,307
Inside, police found firearms,

193
00:10:31,327 --> 00:10:34,730
fuel and explosives. Then the buses arrived.

194
00:10:34,750 --> 00:10:39,132
Up to seven hundred prisoners were transferred out of

195
00:10:39,152 --> 00:10:43,533
Tacumbú that day. Rotela was transferred to a high

196
00:10:43,553 --> 00:10:44,511
security unit.

197
00:10:45,831 --> 00:10:49,247
President Peña spoke with the confidence of a man

198
00:10:49,267 --> 00:10:51,155
who had just won something.

199
00:10:51,175 --> 00:10:52,682
Enough privileges, he said.

200
00:10:52,702 --> 00:10:56,118
We said enough to a prison model that turned

201
00:10:56,138 --> 00:10:58,027
prisons into schools of crime.

202
00:10:59,347 --> 00:11:03,417
And his vice minister added a line aimed at

203
00:11:03,437 --> 00:11:05,689
anyone watching. The Paraguayan state,

204
00:11:05,709 --> 00:11:07,962
he said, respected human rights,

205
00:11:07,982 --> 00:11:10,688
and will not negotiate with criminals.

206
00:11:13,028 --> 00:11:15,767
Chapter 8. After the jungle.

207
00:11:20,367 --> 00:11:25,053
Four days later, Paraguay's congress declared a penitentiary emergency.

208
00:11:25,073 --> 00:11:28,191
It would be extended for years.

209
00:11:29,511 --> 00:11:31,250
The government kept going.

210
00:11:31,270 --> 00:11:33,449
In April twenty twenty four,

211
00:11:33,469 --> 00:11:35,648
in an operation called Joapy,

212
00:11:35,668 --> 00:11:39,606
twenty five Brazilian prisoners linked to the PCC and

213
00:11:39,626 --> 00:11:41,806
other Brazilian gangs were deported.

214
00:11:41,826 --> 00:11:45,764
Later that year, searches seized machetes and phones from

215
00:11:45,784 --> 00:11:47,523
cells across the system.

216
00:11:48,843 --> 00:11:53,536
Rotela himself ended up in the maximum security prison

217
00:11:53,556 --> 00:11:56,155
of Martín Mendoza, in Emboscada,

218
00:11:56,175 --> 00:11:59,297
outside Asunción. In twenty twenty four,

219
00:11:59,317 --> 00:12:01,916
a judge unified his sentences.

220
00:12:01,936 --> 00:12:06,629
According to reports, he is due to stay in

221
00:12:06,649 --> 00:12:09,247
prison until twenty forty six.

222
00:12:10,567 --> 00:12:14,307
But the prison system Rotela left behind was still

223
00:12:14,327 --> 00:12:16,396
bursting. According to InSight Crime,

224
00:12:16,416 --> 00:12:20,155
the Emboscada prison held more than one thousand five

225
00:12:20,175 --> 00:12:23,497
hundred inmates in space for about four hundred.

226
00:12:23,517 --> 00:12:27,257
Only around three hundred and forty of them had

227
00:12:27,277 --> 00:12:28,510
actually been sentenced.

228
00:12:29,830 --> 00:12:33,904
But Tacumbú did not suddenly become a model prison.

229
00:12:33,924 --> 00:12:36,179
In February twenty twenty six,

230
00:12:36,199 --> 00:12:40,273
around three hundred and fifty inmates in one pavilion,

231
00:12:40,293 --> 00:12:42,547
most of them clan members,

232
00:12:42,567 --> 00:12:44,822
burned mattresses and attacked guards.

233
00:12:44,842 --> 00:12:48,916
This time, police ended it in about five minutes.

234
00:12:48,936 --> 00:12:53,010
Paraguayan media called it the clan's first riot in

235
00:12:53,030 --> 00:12:54,375
Tacumbú since Veneratio.

236
00:12:56,715 --> 00:12:59,662
Chapter 9. The end of Tacumbú.

237
00:13:04,262 --> 00:13:05,926
In May twenty twenty six,

238
00:13:05,946 --> 00:13:08,956
the government closed the chapter in its own way.

239
00:13:10,276 --> 00:13:13,987
On May eleventh, in an operation called Umbral,

240
00:13:14,007 --> 00:13:18,185
which means threshold, about four hundred and sixty convicted

241
00:13:18,205 --> 00:13:22,382
prisoners were moved from Tacumbú to the maximum security

242
00:13:22,402 --> 00:13:25,181
prison in Emboscada. Tacumbú was renamed.

243
00:13:25,201 --> 00:13:28,912
It became the National Center for Pretrial Detainees.

244
00:13:28,932 --> 00:13:33,109
From now on, it would hold only people waiting

245
00:13:33,129 --> 00:13:34,042
for trial.

246
00:13:35,362 --> 00:13:37,532
The justice minister, Rodrigo Nicora,

247
00:13:37,552 --> 00:13:40,599
who had been vice minister during Veneratio,

248
00:13:40,619 --> 00:13:42,789
put it simply. With this,

249
00:13:42,809 --> 00:13:46,731
he said, we are closing a chapter of more

250
00:13:46,751 --> 00:13:48,921
than sixty years of Tacumbú.

251
00:13:50,241 --> 00:13:53,402
About one thousand six hundred people remained inside,

252
00:13:53,422 --> 00:13:56,186
all of them, at least on paper,

253
00:13:56,206 --> 00:13:58,174
not yet convicted of anything.

254
00:13:59,494 --> 00:14:01,838
And the problem simply moved.

255
00:14:01,858 --> 00:14:04,202
In November twenty twenty six,

256
00:14:04,222 --> 00:14:08,458
two inmates escaped from the Martín Mendoza prison in

257
00:14:08,478 --> 00:14:12,714
Emboscada, the same maximum security complex that now holds

258
00:14:12,734 --> 00:14:16,024
Rotela and hundreds of men from Tacumbú.

259
00:14:16,044 --> 00:14:19,806
The justice minister went in person to inspect,

260
00:14:19,826 --> 00:14:21,698
and ordered tighter controls.

261
00:14:24,038 --> 00:14:26,904
Chapter 10. Who runs the prison?

262
00:14:31,504 --> 00:14:34,265
Paraguay's story is not unique.

263
00:14:34,285 --> 00:14:37,603
Venezuela lost Tocorón to a gang.

264
00:14:37,623 --> 00:14:42,609
Ecuador lost its prisons to Los Choneros and Los

265
00:14:42,629 --> 00:14:45,390
Lobos. El Salvador built CECOT.

266
00:14:46,710 --> 00:14:48,788
The closest parallel is Tocorón,

267
00:14:48,808 --> 00:14:52,563
in Venezuela. There, the Tren de Aragua gang ran

268
00:14:52,583 --> 00:14:54,660
a prison with a pool,

269
00:14:54,680 --> 00:14:56,757
a zoo and a nightclub.

270
00:14:56,777 --> 00:14:58,854
In September twenty twenty three,

271
00:14:58,874 --> 00:15:02,629
eleven thousand Venezuelan troops went in to take it

272
00:15:02,649 --> 00:15:05,985
back. But the gang's leader was not there.

273
00:15:06,005 --> 00:15:07,662
He had already gone.

274
00:15:08,982 --> 00:15:12,392
Three months later, Paraguay did its own version.

275
00:15:12,412 --> 00:15:14,535
Fewer troops. A smaller prison.

276
00:15:14,555 --> 00:15:16,250
But the same question.

277
00:15:16,270 --> 00:15:20,107
Not how to retake a prison for a day,

278
00:15:20,127 --> 00:15:23,108
but how to stop losing it again.

279
00:15:24,428 --> 00:15:26,315
Paraguay took a different path.

280
00:15:26,335 --> 00:15:29,367
It did not build a giant new prison.

281
00:15:29,387 --> 00:15:32,418
It sent in two thousand police and soldiers,

282
00:15:32,438 --> 00:15:34,325
broke up the clan's base,

283
00:15:34,345 --> 00:15:36,995
moved its leader, and renamed the building.

284
00:15:38,315 --> 00:15:42,158
Whether that is enough is still an open question.

285
00:15:42,178 --> 00:15:43,875
The system remains overcrowded.

286
00:15:43,895 --> 00:15:46,879
Most prisoners are still waiting for trial.

287
00:15:46,899 --> 00:15:49,454
And the gangs that grew inside,

288
00:15:49,474 --> 00:15:51,599
the clan and the PCC,

289
00:15:51,619 --> 00:15:52,887
have not disappeared.

290
00:15:54,807 --> 00:15:56,541
So what do you think?

291
00:15:56,561 --> 00:15:59,698
Can a country take back a prison without fixing

292
00:15:59,718 --> 00:16:01,101
the system around it?

293
00:16:01,121 --> 00:16:02,855
Tell us in the comments.

294
00:16:02,875 --> 00:16:06,013
And for the prison in Venezuela that a gang

295
00:16:06,033 --> 00:16:07,416
turned into a resort,

296
00:16:07,436 --> 00:16:09,521
with a zoo and a pool,

297
00:16:09,541 --> 00:16:11,275
watch our story about Tocorón,

298
00:16:11,295 --> 00:16:12,327
on this channel.
